<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Mencius</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mencius Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> is a temple complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoucheng">Zoucheng</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, dedicated to the philosopher Mencius. First built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> in the 11th century and later moved to its present site, it is a large group of halls and courtyards modelled on the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qufu">Qufu</a>. With the nearby mansion and cemetery of the Meng family it is a protected national monument.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-mencius.jpg" alt="Temple of Mencius" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grand Canyon National Park · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Canyon_DEIS_Aerial_the_Long_Arm_Below_Mencius_Temple_(5477170448).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
